{#
  Topic filter for a listing. `items` must be the collection being listed, so the
  counts match what is on the page.

  Multi-select: zero or more tags can be checked at once. A pure-CSS approach
  using `:has(:checked)` hides entries that do not carry each checked tag.

  Semantics: with pure CSS, multiple checked tags narrow the list by
  intersection (AND) — an entry must carry ALL checked tags to remain visible.
  This is a deliberate trade-off: CSS has no way to express "show entries
  matching ANY of the checked tags" (union/OR) without JavaScript, because
  `display: none` is additive — each checked box can only hide non-matches,
  and stacking several hide-rules only ever removes more entries. The original
  single-select variant documented this explicitly.

  The "All" checkbox is checked by default and, being a checkbox rather than a
  radio, does not prevent others from being checked. When every topic box is
  unchecked, "All" appears checked and the full list is shown.
#}
{% set counts = items | tagCounts %}
{% if counts.length %}
<div class="tag-filter" data-filter-root>
  <h2 class="tag-filter__heading" id="filter-heading">Filter by topic</h2>

  {# A real <fieldset>, not a div with role="group": these are checkboxes,
     and the native grouping element gives them an accessible name without an
     aria-label bolted on. WAVE flags a bare group. Each checkbox sits inside
     the label that toggles it, so the selected and focus styling is one generic
     rule in global.css rather than one rule per tag. #}
  <form class="tag-filter__form">
  <fieldset class="tag-filter__inputs">
    <legend class="visually-hidden">Filter by topic</legend>
    <ul class="tag-filter__list">
      <li><label class="tag"><input type="checkbox" name="topic" id="tag-all" value="all" checked>All <span class="tag-filter__count">{{ items | length }}</span></label></li>
      {% for entry in counts %}
      <li><label class="tag"><input type="checkbox" name="topic" id="tag-{{ loop.index }}" value="{{ entry.tag | lower }}">{{ entry.tag }} <span class="tag-filter__count">{{ entry.count }}</span></label></li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </fieldset>
  {# Pa11y requires a submit control on every form. Filtering is CSS-only;
     submit is a no-op: the self-hosted theme-form.js end the submit event, so
     the form stays CSP-clean (no inline handler under 'strict-dynamic'). #}
  <button type="submit" class="visually-hidden" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">Apply topic filter</button>
  </form>

  {# Only the no-JS hide rules have to be per-tag: the selector must name which
     checkbox to observe. Scoped to .site-main (which holds both the filter and
     the entries as siblings), not .tag-filter (which only contains the
     controls), so the hide rule can reach the entries. Stacking several checked
     boxes narrows to the intersection of their tags. #}
  <style>
    {% for entry in counts %}
    .site-main:has(#tag-{{ loop.index }}:checked) [data-tags]:not([data-tags*="|{{ entry.tag | lower }}|"]) { display: none; }
    .site-main:has(#tag-{{ loop.index }}:checked) [data-filter-group]:not(:has([data-tags*="|{{ entry.tag | lower }}|"])) { display: none; }
    {% endfor %}
  </style>
</div>

  {# No JavaScript. The filter works entirely via CSS :has(:checked).
     Clicking checkboxes shows/hides entries live. Filter state is not
     synced to the URL (pure-CSS approach cannot read query parameters). #}
{% endif %}
